    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24139_english_2026-08-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-06-2026-08-06-0060",
    "chunk_index": 60,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "2f852e3ccc1c7f4f296a0a7eeddf8a75",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1992,
    "chunk_text": "4. The TRCSL has taken steps to revise the existing Broadband Quality of Service Rules to monitor networks of different technologies. With licensing of the new category of satellite service providers, including Starlink, a national strategy will be introduced for universal broadband connectivity in addition to existing programmes, conducting through fixed and mobile operators to serve the unserved and underserved areas.\n\n6. All satellite network providers, including Starlink, are governed by the provisions of Sri Lanka Telecommunications Act Provider Rules published in Extraordinary Gazette No. 2395/34 and the proposed dashboard to be set up to ensure lawful orders of competent authorities.\n\n7. Identical licence fees for same service category of operators and licence fees for all types of provider licences are stipulated in Provider Rules published in Extraordinary Gazette No. 2395/34. Other taxes and statutory levies are same for all operators and providers.\n\n8. MNP implementation roadmap and the operator readiness reports could be finalized based on the outcome of the discussions with operators, as I mentioned in Answer to question No. 1.\n\nThe regulatory framework applicable to satellite operators and the licence conditions applicable to Starlink are included in Provider Rules published in Extraordinary Gazette No. 2395/34.\n\nAlso, the Government's long-term National Telecommunications Competition and Consumer Protection Strategy is in the process of declaring the operator with Significant Market Power - SMP - and introducing obligations to that operator to ensure fair competition. Steps are being taken to introduce models to determine cost-based tariffs. Also, we are hopeful that SMP and the cost models would get out within this year.\n\nThank you. \u0dd4\u0dab \u0d9a\u0dae\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dd9\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bc8\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Speaker) Hon. Ravi Karunanayake, now, it is time for you to seek a clarifications."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-06-2026-08-06",
    "num": "2026-08-06",
    "date_str": "2026-08-06",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-08-06",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24139_english_2026-08-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-06-2026-08-06-0061",
    "chunk_index": 61,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "41d0d3da36ef96b20d7e7ce6d26941c5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1959,
    "chunk_text": "Also, the Government's long-term National Telecommunications Competition and Consumer Protection Strategy is in the process of declaring the operator with Significant Market Power - SMP - and introducing obligations to that operator to ensure fair competition. Steps are being taken to introduce models to determine cost-based tariffs. Also, we are hopeful that SMP and the cost models would get out within this year.\n\nThank you. \u0dd4\u0dab \u0d9a\u0dae\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dd9\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bc8\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Speaker) Hon. Ravi Karunanayake, now, it is time for you to seek a clarifications.\n\nHon. Deputy Minister, one of the prime duties of the Regulator - the Telecommunications Regulatory Commission of Sri Lanka - would be to ensure consumer protection and to give top priority to consumer satisfaction. You were saying that one operator does not want to go ahead and that it should not be a policy change. You, of course, qualified to say that in September it would be put into place. But, the request from that service operator should not have even been entertained because if consumer satisfaction and provision of a quality service come first, I guess the market share of an operator is not what should be determined, but whether the quality of service drops. Today, it takes 35 seconds for a call to be connected and that is the reason why competition should be brought in, which would ensure the necessary consumer protection.\n\n1935 1936\n\n[ \u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d91\u0dbb\u0dda \u0dc0\u0dd3\u0dbb\u0dbb\u0db8\u0d9a\u0db1 \u0dc0\u0dda\u0dca]\n\n2026 \u0db8\u0dc1 \u0dcf\u0dad\u0dd4 06\n\nAlso, Hon. Deputy Minister, is there telephone tapping on WhatsApp or on normal telephones because that is a general issue which many people speak of? When certain things are spoken of, that information gets passed on prior to taking that action, which is a very alarming state. So, I would appreciate it if an honest answer comes from you, taking that matter into consideration."
  },
